  .stratum__plane {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* THE ONE DECLARATION THAT COLOURS THE WHOLE DRAWING. Every stop in the SVG
       is currentColor, so this single line drives the face, the edge, the
       specular highlight and the node. Set here rather than on .stratum so it
       can never leak into the label text. */
    color: var(--hue);
  }

  /* The rail. Absolute across the full row height so consecutive rows abut with
     no seam, and left:50% is exact because the plane cell and the drawing are
     the same width and the node sits at 252 of 504. */
  .stratum__axis {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    left: 50%;
    width: 2px;
    margin-left: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue) 55%, transparent);
  }

  /* THE RAIL STOPS AT THE FIRST AND LAST NODES, with an 18px overshoot that
     fades out. The overshoot is the whole blueprint effect: a rail that stops
     dead on the node reads as a join, a rail that runs 18px past it and fades
     reads as a longer line the drawing has cropped. 50% of the cell is exactly
     the node, because the drawing is centred in the cell and the node is
     centred in the drawing, whatever height the row settles at. */
  .stratum:first-child .stratum__axis {
    top: calc(50% - 18px);
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue) 55%, transparent) 18px
    );
  }

  .stratum:last-child .stratum__axis {
    bottom: calc(50% - 18px);
    background: linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue) 55%, transparent) 18px
    );
  }

  /* TWO-COLOUR GLOW: a tight 12px core in teal under a wide 30px halo in
     teal-dark. Applied to this wrapper rather than to the SVG so the travelling
     light inside the rail is outside the filtered subtree and cannot force the
     blur to be recomputed on every frame.

     BOTH RADII AND THE WHOLE WRAPPER SCALE WITH --lift. That is the elevation
     half of the ladder: a plane that sits forward is brighter AND throws a
     wider glow, which is the same thing light does. The opacity here is safe
     and is not the depth-by-degraded-text failure this component was built to
     avoid: this wrapper contains the aria-hidden SVG and nothing else, and every
     word in the hero sits outside it at a full-contrast token colour. */
  .stratum__glow {
    width: 100%;
    opacity: var(--lift);
    filter: drop-shadow(
        0 0 calc(12px * var(--lift)) color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue) 42%, transparent)
      )
      drop-shadow(0 0 calc(30px * var(--lift)) color-mix(in srgb, var(--hue-halo) 24%, transparent));
  }

  /* width/height attributes are on the element as well, so the drawing has an
     intrinsic size and a reserved aspect ratio even before this rule applies.
     No layout shift, and no dependence on viewBox ratio inference. */
  .plane {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* ── The one animated moment ──────────────────────────────────────────────
     A light falls down the axis, Qualify to Win to Get paid, which is the same
     statement the headline and the stacking order already make. It is:
       - decorative, aria-hidden, and inside a 2px rail that clips it
       - transform and opacity only, so it stays on the compositor
       - absent entirely under prefers-reduced-motion, not merely slowed
     If it never runs, nothing about the hero is missing. That is the rule this
     codebase exists to hold after sv-reveal left 7 of 9 homepage sections
     permanently invisible when its observer did not fire. */
  .stratum__pulse {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .stratum__pulse {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      /* Full rail height, with the visible band drawn inside it, so a
         translateY of 100% is exactly one rail and the travel is predictable
         at any row height. */
      height: 100%;
      background: linear-gradient(
        180deg,
        transparent 28%,
        var(--hue) 50%,
        transparent 72%
      );
      animation: strata-fall 3.9s linear infinite;
    }

    /* One third of the cycle apart, so the light leaves one plane as it reaches
       the next and the three rails read as one descent. */
    .stratum:nth-child(2) .stratum__pulse {
      animation-delay: 1.3s;
    }
    .stratum:nth-child(3) .stratum__pulse {
      animation-delay: 2.6s;
    }
  }

  @keyframes strata-fall {
    from {
      transform: translateY(-100%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
    18% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    82% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    to {
      transform: translateY(100%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ── Phone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     At 390 the content box is 350px. A 320/140/500/200 split of that gives the
     label 96px and the plane 151px, which wraps every line to four words and
     renders a 40px tall diamond. The split is what fails, so it is dropped
     rather than squeezed: label above plane, full width, in reading order.

     The wire and the rail go with it. Both exist to join a left column to a
     right one, and once the rows are stacked the rail would run straight
     through the label text it is supposed to point at. Both are decorative and
     aria-hidden, so removing them removes nothing but decoration. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .stratum {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      row-gap: var(--stack-1);
      padding-block: var(--stack-2);
    }

    .stratum__wire,
    .stratum__axis {
      display: none;
    }
  }
